WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:09.636
This is Orion-X Phoenix Edition, version 2.2.0 beta: a live USB cyberdeck for incident responders working in networks they cannot trust.

00:00:09.636 --> 00:00:15.816
It boots from a USB stick on Debian 13, runs entirely from RAM, and works fully air-gapped.

00:00:15.816 --> 00:00:20.094
Nothing has to be installed on the machine you are standing at.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:24.247
The desktop is XFCE, themed as a cyberdeck.

00:00:24.247 --> 00:00:34.763
The panel shows what matters at a glance: the mesh you are joined to, scan and intrusion events seen on the event bus, and the number of hosts on the local network.

00:00:34.763 --> 00:00:37.712
Every Orion-X tool lives under one Orion menu.

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:42.202
The Control Center is designed for your 3 a.m.

00:00:42.202 --> 00:00:42.538
self.

00:00:42.538 --> 00:00:46.975
Live health, the WireGuard mesh, and the firewall are on one page.

00:00:46.975 --> 00:00:50.874
Threat posture has three tiers, from passive to deception.

00:00:50.874 --> 00:00:51.412
R.A.I.N.

00:00:51.412 --> 00:01:00.688
— Real-time Audible Intrusion Notification — turns detections into severity-mapped sound cues, so an intrusion is heard, not just watched.

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:11.956
Nebula is the on-device assistant: ollama serving Qwen 2.5 3B, verified at boot by a SHA-256 integrity gate before it is allowed to start.

00:01:11.956 --> 00:01:22.107
It can reach twelve local MCP tools — pcap summaries, artifact analysis, OAST decoding, nuclei-template lookups — and nothing leaves the deck.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:36.104
The Cockpit is where you see and hear the cyber activity around you: a scrolling event stream, a threat-pressure gauge that decays over time, a network sparkline, subsystem lights and the current posture.

00:01:36.104 --> 00:01:40.857
This feed is synthetic demo traffic; on a real deck it is the live event bus.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:58.491
Underneath is a curated forensics toolkit: Wireshark and Zeek for the wire, Volatility 3 for memory, capa and YARA for binaries, go-roast for out-of-band payloads, and nucleotide, which maps URLs to nuclei templates from a lookup table built into the image.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:03.069
Pivotglass is the adversary-infrastructure hunting deck.

00:02:03.069 --> 00:02:13.626
It runs locally in the browser, keeps hypotheses, evidence and contradictions as separate records, and ships with an offline learning case so you can practise without any network at all.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:19.259
The full User Guide is rendered into the image and on GitHub.

00:02:19.259 --> 00:02:32.571
The beta ISO is 3.09 gigabytes, published as seven parts because of GitHub's two-gigabyte limit: download them, concatenate, verify the SHA-256, and write the image to an eight-gigabyte stick.

00:02:32.571 --> 00:02:35.067
Beta means we want your bug reports.

00:02:35.067 --> 00:02:36.177
That is Orion-X.
